{"records":[{"id":"6wr2tj8wr9","title":"Phosphoproteomic study of HMMR loss in neuroblastoma cells","description":"This is processed dataset from a mass spectrometric analysis of phosphopeptides extracted from KELLY neuroblastoma cells. It compares peptides from cells with wild type HMMR genes and cells where the HMMR is inactivated using CRISPR/Cas9. The analysis aimed to understand how intracellular signaling was altered after the removal of HMMR to understand the potential functional role of HMMR in these tumour cells. the signalling networks in which HMMR was involved in these tumour cells. The are four cell types used: KELLY (parental), KC17 (subject to CRISPR/Cas9 but with intact HMMR genes), KA5 and KA14 (subjected to CRISPR/Cas9 and with out of frame mutations in HMMR, and not expressing the protein). We examined peptides in KA5 and KA14 that show significantly different phosphorylation from those found in the KELLY and KC17. The MS-482 datafile contain  several outputs including heatmaps, KSEA analyses plus detailed individual peptide data after processing as described in Thompson, E. M., et al. (2022). FEBS Open Bio. DOI: 10.1002/2211-5463.13418. \n\n\t\n\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-03-18T16:48:41.565Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/6wr2tj8wr9","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/6wr2tj8wr9"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"andrew","last_name":"stoker","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/00wx2xr17","name":"Neuroblastoma UK","grant_id":null}],"categories":["Cell Biology","Cancer","Cancer Cell"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"vzr4snpdb4","title":"SR8278 inhibits cell proliferation independent of REV-ERB","description":"SR8278 has been reported to be an antagonist of the REV-ERB proteins. In this work, we treated HaCaT cells with SR8278 and used RNA-seq to identify genes that were significantly altered by treatment. Genes involved in cell proliferation and the G1/S phase transition were a major group of altered genes. We confirmed the RNA-seq results with RT-qPCR and western blotting. Furthermore, SR8278 treatment slowed the growth of HaCaT and other cell lines. Using CRISPR/Cas9 genome editing, we generated REV-ERB alpha, REV-ERB beta, and REV-ERB alpha/beta double-knockout HaCaT cells and found that SR8278's effects were independent of REV-ERB. The data include those generated by RT-qPCR, western blotting, and cell viability assays.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-08-11T15:43:52.8Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vzr4snpdb4","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vzr4snpdb4"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Michael","last_name":"Kemp","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/05dbx6743","name":"Department of Veterans Affairs","grant_id":"I01CX002241"},{"id":"https://ror.org/04q48ey07","name":"National Institute of General Medical Sciences","grant_id":"R01GM130583"},{"id":"https://ror.org/00wzt9y79","name":"Ohio Cancer Research Associates","grant_id":"5020"}],"categories":["Pharmacology","Nuclear Receptor","Transcription Factor","Small Molecule","Keratinocyte"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"3cy28w7v9k","title":"RNA-seq profiling of 634T murine KP NSCLC cells deleted for AMPK subjected to no glucose conditions","description":"This dataset was generated for the paper, \"Genetic analysis reveals AMPK is required to support tumor growth in murine Kras-dependent lung cancer models.\"  Murine Kras mutant, p53 null (KP) 634T NSCLC cells were subjected to the CRISPR/Cas9 system including either a control gRNA (“WT”), or a gRNA targeting both AMPKalpha1 and AMPKalpha2 resulting in AMPK knockout in a polyclonal pooled cell population (“KO”). \"R1\" indicates Replicate 1, \"R2\" indicates Replicate 2. These cells were subjected to no glucose (0mM, \"NG\") conditions for 16 hours, and profiled by RNA-sequencing. \n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-01-10T16:47:21.29Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/3cy28w7v9k","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/3cy28w7v9k"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lillian","last_name":"Eichner","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Reuben","last_name":"Shaw","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Lung Cancer","RNA Sequencing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"rhsw3nbjhz","title":"Essential gene profiles for human pluripotent stem cells identify uncharacterized genes and substrate dependencies. Mair et al.","description":"Human pluripotent stem cells (hPSCs) provide an invaluable tool for modeling diseases and hold promise for regenerative medicine. For understanding pluripotency and lineage differentiation mechanisms, a critical first step involves systematically cataloging genes that are indispensable for hPSC maintenance and proliferation. To map genetic determinants of hPSC fitness, we performed genome-scale loss-of-function screens in an inducible Cas9 H1 hPSC line cultured on feeder cells and feeder-free to identify essential genes. Among these, we found FOXH1 and VENTX, genes that encode transcription factors previously implicated in stem cell biology, as well as an uncharacterized gene, C22orf43/DRICH1. hPSCs essential genes are substantially different from other cell lines, and gene essentiality in hPSCs is highly context-dependent with respect to different growth substrates. Our CRISPR screens establish parameters for genome-wide screens in hPSCs, which will facilitate the characterization of unappreciated regulators of hPSC biology to understand the genetic wiring of hPSC fitness and pluripotency.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-01-24T11:57:50.932Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rhsw3nbjhz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rhsw3nbjhz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Barbara","last_name":"Mair","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jelena","last_name":"Tomic","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sanna","last_name":"Masud","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jason","last_name":"Moffat","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Stem Cell","Functional Genomics","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"4g722x4jfg","title":"Western blot","description":"Western blot of CRISPR-Cas9-mediated nontargeting (NT) versus BACH2 knockout 3 days after CRISPR-Cas9-gRNA nucleofection of human gut cells.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-07-02T21:20:55.966Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/4g722x4jfg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/4g722x4jfg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yulong","last_name":"Wei","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Gut","Western Blot","T Cell","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ydmkfdbt3s","title":"Ovarian Clear Cell Carcinoma:  Clinical and histopathologic features","description":"Clinicopathological findings show that besides lymph node status and advanced stage, intratumoral budding and peritumoral budding are independent predictors of prognosis in ovarian clear cell carcinoma.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-07-19T07:03:52.455Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ydmkfdbt3s","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ydmkfdbt3s"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Pratibha","last_name":"Shukla","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Anatomical Pathology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"8xhv88c7wt","title":"Genome-wide screening in human kidney organoids identifies developmental and disease-related aspects of nephrogenesis","description":"Human organoids allow the study of proliferation, lineage specification, and three-dimensional tissue development. Here, we present a genome-wide CRISPR screen in iPSC-derived kidney organoids. The combination of inducible genome editing, longitudinal sampling and endpoint sorting of tubular and stromal cells generated a complex, high-quality dataset uncovering a broad spectrum of insightful biology from early development to ‘adult’ epithelial morphogenesis. Our functional dataset allows improving mesoderm induction by ROCK inhibition, contains monogenetic and complex trait kidney disease genes, confirms two additional CAKUT genes (CCDC170 and MYH7B), and provides a large candidate list of ciliopathy-related genes. Finally, the identification of a cis-inhibitory effect of Jagged1 controlling epithelial proliferation shows how mosaic knockouts in pooled CRISPR screening can discover ways of communication between heterogeneous cell populations in complex tissues. Collectively, these data serve both as a rich resource for the kidney community and as a benchmark for future iPSC-derived organoid CRISPR screens.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-11-29T13:48:59.335Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/8xhv88c7wt","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/8xhv88c7wt"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Rosemarie","last_name":"Ungricht","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Philipp","last_name":"Hoppe","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Screening","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"j5n84znyvf","title":"Tumor-Educated Hepatocytes Drive Immune Evasion in Colorectal Cancer Liver Metastasis Through KLK5 Secretion","description":"1.RNA-seq data generated from paired primary colorectal cancer lesions and matched hepatic metastatic lesions obtained from the same patients with colorectal cancer liver metastasis (CRLM).\n2.RNA-seq data of THLE-2 cells after co-culture with RKO cells and of control (monocultured) THLE-2 cells.\n3. RNA-seq data of RKO cells after co-culture with THLE-2 cells and of control (monocultured) RKO cells.\n4. LC–MS data for protein identification in conditioned-medium supernatants from control THLE-2 cells and tumor-educated THLE-2 cells.\n5. CRISPR screening for KLK5 substrates.\n6. List of sgRNA sequences used for CRISPR screening.\n7. LC–MS data for the identification of lysine lactylation sites on PD-L1 protein.\n8. LC–MS data for the identification of lysine lactylation sites on PD-L1 protein.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-09-22T17:55:50.043Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/j5n84znyvf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/j5n84znyvf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jiaxi","last_name":"Li","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology","Metastatic Colorectal Cancer"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"2v5jg2zksj","title":"TMEM79/MATTRIN defines a pathway for Frizzled regulation and is required for Xenopus embryogenesis","description":"Absctract\nWnt signaling through the Frizzled (FZD) family of serpentine receptors is essential for embryogenesis and homeostasis, and stringent control of the FZD protein level is critical for stem cell regulation. Through CRISPR/Cas9 genome-wide screening in human cells, we identified TMEM79/MATTRIN, an orphan multi-span transmembrane protein, as a specific inhibitor of Wnt/FZD signaling. TMEM79 interacts with FZD during biogenesis and promotes FZD degradation independent of ZNRF3/RNF43 ubiquitin ligases (R-spondin receptors). TMEM79 interacts with ubiquitin-specific protease 8 (USP8), whose activating mutations underlie human tumorigenesis. TMEM79 specifically inhibits USP8 deubiquitination of FZD, thereby governing USP8 substrate specificity and promoting FZD degradation. Tmem79 and Usp8 genes have a pre-bilaterian origin, and Tmem79 inhibition of Usp8 and Wnt signaling is required for anterior neural development and gastrulation in Xenopus embryos. TMEM79 is a predisposition gene for Atopic dermatitis, suggesting deregulation of Wnt/FZD signaling a possible cause for this most common yet enigmatic inflammatory skin disease.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-09-26T05:47:58.606Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/2v5jg2zksj","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/2v5jg2zksj"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Maorong","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nathalia","last_name":"Amado","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jieqiong","last_name":"Tan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alice ","last_name":"Reis","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mengxu","last_name":"Ge","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jose","last_name":"Abreu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xi","last_name":"He","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry","Molecular Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"sxrvzkr4hh","title":"Pooled in vitro and in vivo CRISPR-Cas9 screening identifies tumor suppressors in human colon organoids","description":"UMI validation screen, original data from in vivo tumor suppressor CRISPR/Cas9 screening experiment. Figure 6.\nExperimental setup: 281 tumor suppressor gRNAs, positive controls, neutral controls and gRNAs targeting essential genes were introduced into the CRISPR-UMI lentivirus (Michlits et al., 2017). Transduced AK organoids (APC-KO and KRASG12D) were transplanted subcutaneously in NSG mice followed by barcode sequencing of the tumor pool (from 10 mice) and 5 individual tumors. Clone and read number were determined by next generation sequencing. The conventional gRNA enrichment analysis was compared to UMI-CRISPR-screening with outlier removal.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-04-20T15:46:17.868Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/sxrvzkr4hh","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/sxrvzkr4hh"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Henner","last_name":"Farin","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":141408,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":168,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":141408,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":140698,"restricted":69,"embargoed":641},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2340},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1346},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":682},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":475},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":439},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":426},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":415},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":388},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":333},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":282}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":138749},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1909},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":186},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":137},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":79},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":59},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":41},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":32},{"id":"f2ed6517-7fb4-4e02-8cef-95e75e9f0c9d","name":"Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University","count":28}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":872},{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":774},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":529},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":409},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":380}]}}